[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **single‑file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`) that makes a `pmtiles://` URL work with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why “once”?**  
> The protocol registry is global. Registering it a second time or inside a component’s render loop can cause duplicate handlers or memory leaks.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already contains the compatibility shim that works with the new promise‑based `addProtocol` signature, so you don’t have to write your own async handler.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### 3‑a️⃣ Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // One source per .pmtiles file
      world: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol handler reads the archive header and returns a TileJSON
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Add more layers (roads, labels, …) that reference the same source
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### 3‑b️⃣ Raster imagery

```js
{
  sources: {
    imagery: {
      type: 'raster',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/satellite.pmtiles',
      tileSize: 256               // optional, defaults to 256
    }
  },
  layers: [
    {
      id: 'sat',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'imagery',
      paint: {}
    }
  ]
}
```

### 3‑c️⃣ Raster‑DEM (terrain)

```js
{
  sources: {
    terrain: {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation.pmtiles',
      encoding: 'terrarium'      // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  terrain: {
    source: 'terrain',
    exaggeration: 1.5
  }
}
```

> **Important:** Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`). The PMTiles protocol reads the archive header, extracts the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and returns a TileJSON for MapLibre. If you use a `tiles:` template you lose that automatic zoom‑range handling and MapLibre may request zoom levels that don’t exist in the file.

---

## 4️⃣ Host the `.pmtiles` file where it can be fetched with **HTTP Range** requests

| Provider | How to enable |
|----------|---------------|
| **AWS S3** | Public read or signed URL; S3 already supports `Range`. Set `Cache‑Control: public, max‑age=31536000` and `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *`. |
| **Cloudflare R2** | Same as S3 (S3‑compatible API). Put behind Cloudflare CDN for fast global delivery. |
| **GitHub Pages** | Works for files ≤ 100 MiB. Enable CORS via a `CORS` file or a custom domain with proper headers. |
| **Netlify / Vercel / any static host** | Verify that `Accept‑Ranges: bytes` is returned and that CORS allows `Range`. |

If you get a **CORS** error in the console, add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range` to the response headers.

---

## 5️⃣ (Optional) Clean‑up when the map is destroyed

```js
map.on('remove', () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
});
```

In a React app you’d typically call `removeProtocol` in a `useEffect` cleanup that runs when the last map component unmounts.

---

## 6️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML + ES modules)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, and you should see the water polygons from the `world.pmtiles` archive.

---

## 7️⃣ Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank tiles / no data** | Used `tiles:` instead of `url:` or the archive’s `maxzoom` is lower than the map’s zoom. | Switch to `url:`; optionally set `maxzoom` in the style to a value ≤ archive’s max. |
| **CORS error** | Host does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. | Add those headers on the static host or serve via a CDN that injects them. |
| **404 on tile request** | The host does not support HTTP Range (e.g., a generic web server that strips the `Range` header). | Move the file to S3, R2, Cloudflare Workers KV, or any server that returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| **MapLibre v3 → v4 error** (`callback is not a function`) | You wrote a custom handler using the old callback signature. | Use the built‑in `pmtilesProtocol.tile` (it adapts) or rewrite the handler to `async (params, abortController) => ({data})`. |
| **Large file (> 100 MiB) on GitHub Pages** | GitHub Pages refuses to serve it. | Host the file on S3/R2 or split the archive into regional pieces. |

---

## 8️⃣ TL;DR – One‑liner for a vector source

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);

new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      myData: { type: 'vector', url: 'pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/myData.pmtiles' }
    },
    layers: [{ id: 'myLayer', type: 'fill', source: 'myData', 'source-layer': 'my_layer', paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff6600' } }]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 3
});
```

That’s it—your `.pmtiles` archive is now a first‑class tile source, served from any static bucket, with zero server‑side code required. Happy mapping!

## Yes – you can serve MapLibre tiles from **GitHub Pages** with **zero backend**.  
The trick is to put the tiles in a **PMTiles** archive (a single `.pmtiles` file) and let the browser fetch the needed byte‑ranges directly from the static site.

Below is a complete, step‑by‑step guide:

| What you’ll do | Why it matters |
|----------------|----------------|
| **Create a PMTiles file** (vector, raster or raster‑dem) | One file contains the whole tile pyramid, so you only need to upload a single asset. |
| **Upload the file to a GitHub Pages repo** | GitHub Pages serves static files and supports HTTP `Range` requests, which PMTiles relies on. |
| **Add the `pmtiles` protocol handler to your MapLibre app** | MapLibre doesn’t understand PMTiles natively; the handler makes `pmtiles://…` URLs work. |
| **Reference the file in your style with `url:` (not `tiles:`)** | The protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`. |
| **Make sure CORS and cache headers are correct** | Browsers need `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` and `Accept‑Ranges: bytes`. GitHub Pages already provides both. |

---

## 1️⃣ Generate a PMTiles archive

You can start from any source (OSM, GeoJSON, Shapefile, raster imagery, DEM, …).  
Two common ways:

### A. Direct PMTiles output (tippecanoe ≥ 2.17)

```bash
# Example: a vector tile set from a GeoJSON file
tippecanoe -zg -o mytiles.pmtiles mydata.geojson
```

### B. Convert an existing MBTiles file

```bash
# Install the official CLI (single binary, no npm needed)
#   https://github.com/protomaps/go-pmtiles/releases
pmtiles convert mydata.mbtiles mytiles.pmtiles
```

> **Tip:** Run `pmtiles show mytiles.pmtiles` to verify the header (bounds, min/max zoom, tile count).

---

## 2️⃣ Put the file on GitHub Pages

1. **Create (or use) a repo** that is already configured for GitHub Pages (e.g. `username.github.io` or `username.github.io/your‑project`).  
2. **Add the `.pmtiles` file** to the repo.  
   * If the file is **≤ 100 MiB**, you can commit it directly.  
   * For larger files you have two options:  
     * **Git Large File Storage (LFS)** – works with Pages, but you must enable LFS in the repo and the file will be stored in GitHub’s LFS bucket.  
     * **GitHub Releases** – upload the archive as a release asset and reference the *raw* URL (`https://github.com/username/repo/releases/download/v1.0/mytiles.pmtiles`). The raw URL also supports range requests.  
3. **Push** the commit (or publish the release). GitHub Pages will automatically serve the file at a URL like:

```
https://username.github.io/your-project/mytiles.pmtiles
# or, for a release asset:
https://github.com/username/repo/releases/download/v1.0/mytiles.pmtiles
```

### Verify that range requests work

Open the browser console and run:

```js
fetch('https://username.github.io/your-project/mytiles.pmtiles', {
  headers: { Range: 'bytes=0-1023' }
})
  .then(r => console.log(r.status, r.headers.get('Accept-Ranges')))
  .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
  .then(buf => console.log('got', buf.byteLength, 'bytes'));
```

You should see **status 206** and `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. If you get **200** with the whole file, the server isn’t honoring the `Range` header – GitHub Pages does, so you’re good.

---

## 3️⃣ Add the PMTiles protocol to your MapLibre app

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl pmtiles
```

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (run once, e.g. in index.js or a root React component)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol instance and register it globally
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

// Optional cleanup (e.g. on app unload)
// maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Why the one‑liner works on all MapLibre versions**  
> `protocol.tile` is a shim that adapts to the `addProtocol` signature used by v3, v4, v5, and v6. You don’t need to write your own async handler unless you have a custom source.

---

